Content (CHARTED — Will's direction, main chat, 2026-11-24 15:38 CST):
inky — the writing tool assistant that shipped today to wbic16/inky (commit 0a1e1e56) — ported to iOS.

The engine is one self-contained stdlib Python file of pure deterministic text logic, so the port itself is not the obstacle (a Swift port of the engine, or an embedded-Python shell, are both straightforward shapes). Product shape, as on Android: a keyboard — on iOS, a keyboard extension.

Prerequisite, named at identification (opportunity-identification discipline): an Apple build machine and a test device. Xcode runs only on macOS, and a keyboard extension can only be truly tested on a real iPhone. Neither is in the fleet: no Mac of any kind, no iPhone. The minimum kit, sized in the main chat 2026-11-24 15:37 CST: a used Apple-silicon Mac mini (M1 or better, ~$300-400 — it would serve as the ranch's iOS build node) and a used iPhone 12 or newer (~$200) as a test device. Will named the need himself today, 15:37 CST: "i need an iPhone and a Mac..."

Hop-check on listing (charter hop rule, Will 2026-11-24 15:05 CST): NOT REACHABLE with current assets. The block is exact and singular: no Apple hardware in the fleet — no Mac to run Xcode, no iPhone to test on. Every other ingredient exists (the engine, the design, the Android rung's proof of shape). The hop waits on the hardware decision, which is Will's: acquire the ~$500 used kit (Mac mini build node + test iPhone) and this star's first rung opens. Until then it stays charted as a Moonshot in waiting, its prerequisite on the record.
